# Spacing

Use the Civica spacing scale in a maintained custom card. Every step is a multiple of 5px. Civica has no spacing variable, so write the value in the card's CSS.

## Spacing scale

| Unit | Value | Use it for |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | 5px | Between an icon and its label |
| 2 | 10px | Between a label and its hint, and between items in a row |
| 3 | 15px | Inside a compact card, and between rows |
| 4 | 20px | Inside a card |
| 5 | 25px | Between groups in a busy card |
| 6 | 30px | Between related groups |
| 7 | 40px | Between sections of a custom panel |
| 8 | 50px | Between sections of a custom panel on a wall panel |
| 9 | 60px | Around the main heading of a custom panel |

<%= colour_example('layout-spacing-scale', callout: false) %>

This CSS gives a custom card 20px padding and 30px between 2 groups of controls.

```css
ha-card {
  padding: 20px;
}
.civica-group + .civica-group {
  margin-top: 30px;
}
```

## Spacing on narrow screens

Use smaller spacing on a phone, so more of the card fits on the screen. The Civica house lighting card steps down on screens up to 640px wide.

| Space | Up to 640px | From 641px |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Inside the card | 15px | 20px |
| Below the list of rooms | 20px | 30px |

```css
.body { padding: 15px; }
.rooms { margin-bottom: 20px; }

@media (min-width: 641px) {
  .body { padding: 20px; }
  .rooms { margin-bottom: 30px; }
}
```

Keep 5px, 10px and 15px the same on every screen. They are already small.

### Native Home Assistant spacing

Home Assistant spaces its own cards with `--ha-space-1` to `--ha-space-20`, which go up in steps of 4px. Leave these as they are. Civica does not override native padding to fit its 5px scale, because that would break the native layout.
